  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1036 PM MDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nUpper high will continue to spin over the desert southwest Friday. \nStill have plenty of moisture across the area for another round of \nmonsoonal thunderstorms even with the upper high right over us. Hi-\nres guidance is showing a bit better coverage for storms tomorrow \nwith scattered storms possible everywhere, even in the desert \nlowlands later that afternoon. In addition, Friday will be the \nhottest day of the week ahead with a forecast high of 108F for El \nPaso. Basically all zones except the area mountains will be under a \nHeat Advisory tomorrow. Heading into Saturday, an active \nthunderstorm day looks to be ahead. A cutoff low will dive south \nand east over the mid-western states Saturday which will spill in\na cold front into the area. High pressure will be shunted to the \nwest over AZ that day leaving us under northerly flow. SPC is \nhighlighting much of Otero county, northeast portions of Dona Ana \nand eastern portion of Sierra county in the Marginal Risk (level \n1/5). Severe hail, severe wind gusts, and flash flooding will be \nthe primary hazards. In addition, WPC has the same general area \nunder a Slight Risk (level 2/4) for flash flooding Saturday. \n\nSunday through mid-week next week the upper high will continue to \nmeander over the desert southwest. Slightly cooler over the weekend \nbut temperatures start rising back up to triple digits Monday \nthrough Wednesday. Continued daily thunderstorm chances each \nafternoon with the area mountains and western locations border \nArizona will be favored for storm activity.",
